The warmup timer's catch block silently swallows all exceptions. Per project conventions, catch blocks must at minimum log at debug level before continuing. Even for non-fatal paths, diagnostics are valuable. Change to:
```typescript
} catch (err) {
  logger.debug(`[reactComponentMeta] Warmup extraction failed: ${err}`);
}
```

The catch block discards the actual error and emits a hardcoded message. If TypeScript is installed but fails for a different reason (e.g. version mismatch, corrupted install), the user gets a misleading 'TypeScript not available' message with no diagnostic information. Capture and log the real error:
```typescript
} catch (err) {
  logger.debug(
    `[reactComponentMeta] Could not create ComponentMetaManager, skipping: ${err}`
  );
}
```

`this.commandLine.fileNames.includes(fileName)` inside a loop is O(n) per file, making `ensureFiles` O(n²) for large projects. Since `fileNames` is mutated in place, consider maintaining a companion `Set<string>` (or converting to `Set` for the check) to keep membership tests O(1):
```typescript
const existing = new Set(this.commandLine.fileNames);
for (const fileName of fileNames) {
  if (!existing.has(fileName)) {
    this.commandLine.fileNames.push(fileName);
    existing.add(fileName);
    added = true;
  }
}
```
